===Etymology 1===

====Verb====
{{head|en|verb form}}

# {{infl of|en|dig||ed-form}} ({{n-g|replacing earlier}} [[digged]])

=====Derived terms=====
{{col|en|dugway|undug}}

===Etymology 2===
From earlier {{m|en|dugge}} {{gloss|"pap, teat"; compare also English dialectal {{m|en|ducky}}, {{m|en|dukky||the female breast}}}}, apparently connected to Danish {{m|da|dægge||to suckle}}, Swedish {{m|sv|dägga||to suck}}, Old English {{m|ang|deon|dēon|to suckle}}. More at {{m|en|doe}}.
Compare also {{m|en|doug}}.{{attn|en}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|in the plural}} A [[mammary gland]] on a domestic mammal with more than two [[breast]]s.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|5|II|text=He did comply with his '''dug''' before he sucked it.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1820|author=w:Percy Bysshe Shelley|title=Oedipus Tyrannus; Or, Swellfoot The Tyrant: A Tragedy in Two Acts
|passage={{smc|First Pig.}}<br>I suck, but no milk will come from the '''dug'''.}}
# {{lb|en|vulgar|chiefly|in the plural}} A woman's breast or [[nipple]].

